id: rust.timeout
kind: rust
title: Timeouts
aliases: timeout, deadline, tokio::time::timeout
tags: async, resilience
questions:
  - how do you add a timeout to an async call?
invariant: A timeout bounds how long the caller waits; it does not guarantee the underlying work stopped.
answer_30s: Wrap the future in tokio::time::timeout or race a sleep in select!, and treat the timeout as its own error because the work may still be running.
answer_2m: |
  The subtlety that earns points: the timeout cancels the local future, but the
  remote side may still be executing, so a retry can duplicate the operation --
  timeouts and retries must be paired with idempotency rather than applied
  independently. Choose the value from the caller's remaining budget, not from a
  round number, and make inner timeouts strictly smaller than outer ones,
  otherwise an inner layer outlives the caller that already gave up. Track
  timeouts separately from errors in metrics: a cascade usually shows as rising
  timeouts, and it needs a different response than a rising error rate.
when_to_use:
  - every outbound call to another service, database or queue
avoid_when:
  - as a substitute for fixing a slow dependency (failing faster is still failing)
minimal_example: |
  let res = tokio::time::timeout(Duration::from_millis(300), client.get(url)).await;
  match res {
      Ok(Ok(body)) => Ok(body),
      Ok(Err(e)) => Err(AppError::Upstream(e)), // real error
      Err(_elapsed) => Err(AppError::Timeout),  // budget exhausted
  }
failure_modes:
  - timeout plus retry without idempotency, duplicating a write
  - an inner timeout longer than the caller's deadline, so work continues after the caller gave up
  - no timeout at all, so one stuck dependency exhausts the connection pool
related:
  - rust.tokio-select
  - design.timeouts
  - design.retry-backoff
  - design.circuit-breaker
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/time/fn.timeout.html
